<cfsilent>

<!---
		SCRIPT NAME:		cryp.cfm
		SCRIPT TYPE:		custom tag
		DATE:				May 29, 2001
		PROGRAMMER:			Jackson Moore (jackson@iology.com)

		DESCRIPTION:

		This tag encrypts or decrypts a string of text by adding additional safeguards to the standard ENCRYPT and DECRYPT functions in Cold Fusion.  I use this tag to encrypt sensitive data that is passed as hidden form fields or URL paramaters to prevent users from playing around with the variables and getting access to unauthorized material or causing problems for the code.  Using the standard encryption functions in Cold Fusion can allow hackers to modify the encrypted string that will, when decrypted, produce a value different from the original.  In addition, the standard ENCRYPT function produces an ugly string with characters that can be illegal or cause probelems in URL strings (ampersands, commas, spaces, carriage returns, etc.).  This tag replaces each character with a hexidecimal value so the resulting encrypted string will only contain letters and numbers.  This tag will only function during the start execution mode even if there is an end tag present.

		Although this algorithm can be broken, it provides a substantial amount of increased security for data integrity over the default CF encryption functions.  Users of this code are completely responsible for using it where it makes sense.  This is not a replacement for SSL, RSA or "uncrackable" encryption methods.  The user bears full responsibility.  This tag will, however, increase the security of the standard CF functions and address other drawbacks.

		The actual algorithm uses the standard CF functions but takes the resulting string and encodes it further by replacing all characters in the default encrypted string with hexidecimal values that have been shifted left 1 bit and nibbles flipped.  It also adds a checksum so the decrypted value can be compared to the checksum of the encrypted value for additional assurance of data integrity.  The checksum is also encoded in the new encryption string to reduce the potential for successful tampering.

		The resuling encryption string generated by this tag will be a little longer than twice the length of the default ENCRYPT function since all values are converted to 2 digit hexidecimal values and the checksum is added.  This can result in VERY long URL strings if used indiscriminately, but I haven't had any problems.

		USAGE:				<cf_cryp	[ type = "{ en* | de }" ] (en=encrypt, de=decrypt; default is "en")
										string = "{ string to encrypt or decrypt }"
										key = "{ key to use for encrypt or decrypt }"
										[ return = "{ name a variable to return to the calling page as a structure, default is 'cryp' }"
							>
							* Indicates default values.

		This tag returns a structure to the calling page called "cryp" unless overridden by setting the "return" attribute of this tag.  This is a list of the values returned in this structure.  The default variable "cryp" is assumed, but you can use whatever you like by setting the "return" attribute of this tag.

					cryp.string				original string passed to tag
					cryp.key				original key passed to tag
					cryp.value				encrypted or descrypted result depending on tag usage
					cryp.error				true or false indicating an error
					cryp.errorType			either "unknown" or "cryp"; an errorType of "cryp" indicates a hack attempt
					cryp.errorMessage		actual text of CF error message unless cryp.errorType is "cryp"

		If cryp.errorType is "cryp", you can assume someone modified the values in a hack attempt.  cryp.errorMessage will give you additional details on the type of error in case you want to log these events.

					cryp.errorMessage="nochecksum"			indicates no checksum value in decrypted string
					cryp.errorMessage="badstring"			indicates invalid decrypted string
					cryp.errorMessage="decryptionfail"		indicates that the default DECRYPT function failed to return a valid string
					cryp.errorMessage="checksumfail"		indicates that decrypted string's checksum doesn't match the original

		LICENSE: Users may use this code in whole or in part for any purpose, commercial or otherwise, without citing any credits.

		REVISION HISTORY:	v1.0.0		May 29, 2001		Original Release
--->

<!--- Initialize default values. --->
<cfparam name="Attributes.string" default="">
<cfparam name="Attributes.key" default="">
<cfparam name="Attributes.type" default="en">
<cfparam name="Attributes.return" default="cryp">

<!--- Only process this script if we're being called as a custom tag and the required attributes are present. --->
<cfif isdefined("THISTAG.EXECUTIONMODE") and len(Attributes.string) is not 0 and len(Attributes.key) is not 0>

	<!--- Determines which code to run based on whether we're encountering a start or end tag. --->
	<cfswitch expression="#THISTAG.EXECUTIONMODE#">

		<cfcase value="start">
		<!--- Code to execute on start tag. --->

			<!--- Rename value and key for readability. --->
			<cfset string=Attributes.string>
			<cfset key=Attributes.key>

			<!--- Create return variable and default settings. --->
            <cfset caller[ attributes.return ] = { string: string
                                                   , key: key
                                                   , value: ''
                                                   , error: false
                                                   , errorType: ''
                                                   , errorMessage: ''
                                                }>

            <cfif application.util.query.get_setting('Custom_RFP_Process',0) eq 1>     <!--- no encryption/decryption for Huhtamaki --->
                <cfset caller[ attributes.return ].value = string>
                <cfreturn>
            </cfif>

			<!--- Determine whether we are encrypting or decrypting a string. --->
			<cfswitch expression="#Attributes.type#">

				<cfcase value="de">
				<!--- Decrypt string. --->

					<cftry>

						<!--- Extract check sum and "clean" encryption string. A "J" should delimit the check sum and encrypted value. --->



						<cfif findnocase("J",string,1) is 0>
							<cfthrow type="cryp" message="nochecksum">
						<cfelse>
							<cftry>
								<!--- Get the modified check sum from the string. --->
								<cfset stringCheckSum=mid(string,1,(findnocase("J",string,1)-1))>
								<!--- Re-create the actual check sum by flipping the first and second halves. --->
								<cfset halfLen=len(stringCheckSum)/2>
								<cfset stringCheckSum=mid(stringCheckSum,halfLen+1,halfLen)&mid(stringCheckSum,1,halfLen)>
								<!--- Get the "clean" encrypted string. --->
								<cfset stringClean=right(string,len(string)-findnocase("J",string,1))>
								<cfcatch type="any">
									<cfthrow type="cryp" message="badstring">
								</cfcatch>
							</cftry>
						</cfif>

						<!--- If the "clean" encryption is empty or doesn't have an even number of characters, throw error. --->
						<cfif len(stringClean) is 0 or (len(stringClean) mod 2 is not 0)>
							<cfthrow type="cryp" message="badstring">
						</cfif>

						<!--- Determine "raw" encryption from "clean" encryption. --->
						<cfset stringRaw="">
						<cfloop index="i" from="1" to="#len(stringClean)#" step="2">
							<cftry>
								<!--- Get the hex value and flip it to re-create the actual hex value. --->
								<cfset thisCharHexFlip=mid(stringClean,i,2)>
								<cfset thisCharHex=mid(thisCharHexFlip,2,1)&mid(thisCharHexFlip,1,1)>
								<!--- Get the characater after converting to decimal and shifting right by 1 bit. --->
								<cfset thisChar=chr(bitshrn(inputbasen(thisCharHex,16),1))>
								<cfcatch type="any">
									<cfthrow type="cryp" message="badstring">
								</cfcatch>
							</cftry>
							<!--- Add this "raw" character to the "raw" encryption string. --->
							<cfset stringRaw=stringRaw&thisChar>
						</cfloop>

						<!--- Determine original value. --->
						<cftry>
							<cfset original=decrypt(stringRaw,key)>
							<cfcatch type="any">
								<cfthrow type="cryp" message="decryptionfail">
							</cfcatch>
						</cftry>

						<!--- Determine check sum of original value. --->
						<cfset originalCheckSum=0>
						<cfloop index="i" from="1" to="#len(original)#">
							<cfset thisCharVal=asc(mid(original,i,1))>
							<cfset originalCheckSum=originalCheckSum+thisCharVal>
						</cfloop>

						<!--- Check to see if the check sum matches the original value. --->
						<cfif originalCheckSum is not stringCheckSum>
							<cfthrow type="cryp" message="checksumfail">
						</cfif>

						<!--- Create the return variable. --->
						<cfset setvariable("caller.#Attributes.return#.value",original)>

						<cfcatch type="cryp">
							<!--- Any error with type="cryp" is a warning of a hack attempt. --->
							<cfset setvariable("caller.#Attributes.return#.error","true")>
							<cfset setvariable("caller.#Attributes.return#.errorType","decrypt")>
							<cfset setvariable("caller.#Attributes.return#.errorMessage",cfcatch.message)>
						</cfcatch>

						<cfcatch type="any">
							<!--- Any other error is a CF server or request error. --->
							<cfset setvariable("caller.#Attributes.return#.error","true")>
							<cfset setvariable("caller.#Attributes.return#.errorType","unknown")>
							<cfset setvariable("caller.#Attributes.return#.errorMessage",cfcatch.message)>
						</cfcatch>

					</cftry>




				</cfcase><!--- End Attributes.type="de" --->

				<cfdefaultcase><!--- Same as value="en" --->
				<!--- Encrypt string. --->

					<cftry>

						<!--- Calculate the checksum of the original message using the ASCII value for each character. --->
						<cfset stringCheckSum=0>
						<cfloop index="i" from="1" to="#len(string)#">
							<cfset thisCharVal=asc(mid(string,i,1))>
							<cfset stringCheckSum=stringCheckSum+thisCharVal>
						</cfloop>

						<!--- For some mild security, modify the checksum by flipping the first and second half of the check sum. --->
						<!--- Add a leading 0 to the check sum if the check sum doesn't have an even number of characters. --->
						<cfif len(stringCheckSum) mod 2 is 1>
							<cfset stringCheckSum="0"&stringCheckSum>
						</cfif>
						<!--- Then, we'll modify the check sum by flipping the first and second halves. --->
						<cfset halfLen=len(stringCheckSum)\2>
						<cfset stringCheckSum=mid(stringCheckSum,halfLen+1,halfLen)&mid(stringCheckSum,1,halfLen)>

						<!--- Get the "raw" default CF encrypted text. --->
						<cfset stringRaw=encrypt(string,key)>

						<!--- Create the "clean" encrypted text. --->
						<!--- This algorithm replaces each raw character with it's hex value, shifted 1 bit left and flipped. --->
						<cfset stringClean="">
						<cfloop index="i" from="1" to="#len(stringRaw)#">
							<!--- Get the hex value of the ASCII value of the character shifted 1 bit to the left. --->
							<cfset thisCharHex=formatbasen(bitshln(asc(mid(stringRaw,i,1)),1),16)>
							<!--- Insert leading 0 if the hex value is only 1 character. --->
							<cfif len(thisCharHex) is 1>
								<cfset thisCharHex=0&thisCharHex>
							</cfif>
							<!--- Flip the upper and lower nibbles of the bit. --->
							<cfset thisCharHexFlip=mid(thisCharHex,2,1)&mid(thisCharHex,1,1)>
							<!--- Add this new value to the "clean" encrypted string. --->
							<cfset stringClean=stringClean&ucase(thisCharHexFlip)>
						</cfloop>

						<!--- Create the return variable with encrypted text and original checksum. The "J" is a non-obvious delimiter. --->
						<cfset value=stringCheckSum&"J"&stringClean>
						<cfset setvariable("caller.#Attributes.return#.value",value)>

						<cfcatch type="any">
							<!--- If any error occurs, set the error variable. --->
							<cfset setvariable("caller.#Attributes.return#.error","true")>
							<cfset setvariable("caller.#Attributes.return#.errorType","unknown")>
							<cfset setvariable("caller.#Attributes.return#.errorMessage",cfcatch.message)>
						</cfcatch>

					</cftry>

				</cfdefaultcase><!--- End Attributes.type="en" --->

			</cfswitch><!--- End cfswitch on Attributes.type --->

		</cfcase><!--- End THISTAG.EXECUTIONMODE="start" --->

	</cfswitch><!--- End cfswitch on THISTAG.EXECUTIONMODE --->

</cfif>

</cfsilent>
